WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 164: Woodrow Wilson: The Scholar in Politics and the New Freedom (1913–1916)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.840
[Rebecca] Today, we step into the presidency of Woodrow Wilson.

4
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:17.280
[Rebecca] He was an intellectual of soaring rhetorical power, unbending moral rectitude, and fierce administrative determination.

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:26.720
[Rebecca] Arriving in the executive mansion in 1913, Wilson presided over the most sweeping transformation of the American economic system since the Civil War.

6
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story of Woodrow Wilson and the new freedom.

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:42.640
[George] Thomas Woodrow Wilson was born in Stanton, Virginia, in 1856, the son of a distinguished Presbyterian theologian.

8
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:53.120
[George] Raised in the war torn South during the Civil War and Reconstruction, Wilson grew up in Augusta, Georgia, and Columbia, South Carolina, where he watched General Robert E.

9
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.560
[George] Lee pass in review and witnessed firsthand the devastating wreckage of military defeat.

10
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:08.880
[George] Wilson struggled with severe dyslexia as a child, learning to read only at age 10, but overcame his disability through ferocious discipline and shorthand writing.

11
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:20.160
[George] Backslash N Backslash and educated at Princeton University and the University of Virginia Law School, Wilson despised practicing law, which he found narrow and commercial.

12
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:28.240
[George] Instead, he enrolled at Johns Hopkins University, earning a doctorate in political science and history in 1886.

13
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:35.760
[George] To this day, Woodrow Wilson remains the only president of the United States to hold a PhD his doctoral dissertation.

14
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:47.440
[George] Congressional government was an instant classic, analyzing the American constitutional system with surgical brilliance and arguing that the presidency should function like a British prime minister.

15
00:01:47.440 --> 00:02:04.760
[George] Leading the legislative branch through rhetorical persuasion and moral vision, backslash and backslash and Wilson's Presbyterian upbringing instilled in him an unshakable sense of moral destiny and a belief that political leadership was a sacred calling to advance justice and human welfare.

16
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:13.080
[George] He viewed the presidency not merely as an administrative office, but as a spiritual pulpit from which to guide the nation toward righteousness.

17
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.920
[George] In 1902, Wilson was unanimously elected president of Princeton University.

18
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:33.720
[George] He immediately revolutionized the institution, firing incompetent professors, overhauling the curriculum, and establishing the innovative preceptorial system of small discussion groups that became the gold standard of American higher education.

19
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:49.880
[George] Yet when Wilson attempted to abolish the university's exclusive, wealthy eating clubs, which he considered undemocratic and elitist, he collided with wealthy alumni and trustees, fighting a bitter, exhausting civil war that taught him the brutal mechanics of politics.

20
00:02:53.080 --> 00:03:02.360
[George] New Jersey Democratic Party bosses, seeking a respectable, scholarly figurehead to front their corrupt machine, nominated Wilson for governor.

21
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.040
[George] The bosses assumed the naive professor would do their bidding.

22
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.280
[George] They could not have been more mistaken.

23
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.720
[George] Upon taking the oath, Governor Wilson turned on the machine with ruthless efficiency.

24
00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:30.040
[George] In a single legislative session, Wilson passed sweeping direct primary laws, a corrupt practices act, utility regulation, and workmen's compensation, turning New Jersey from a notorious corporate haven into a national laboratory of progressive reform.

25
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:35.640
[George] Two years later, the Democratic Party nominated him for president on the 46th ballot.

26
00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:47.280
[George] Backslash and backslash and Wilson's triumph over the New Jersey political machine proved that a scholar could outmaneuver seasoned party bosses by appealing directly to the moral conscience of the voters.

27
00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:56.880
[George] His executive success in Trenton demonstrated that academic intellect, when combined with political will, could dismantle corrupt party monopolies.

28
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:07.600
[George] Taking office in March 1913, following the Republican split between Taft and Roosevelt, Wilson immediately announced his intention to lead Congress like a prime minister.

29
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:20.480
[George] In 1801, Thomas Jefferson had discontinued the practice of delivering presidential addresses to Congress in person, considering it an aristocratic imitation of the British monarch's speech from the throne.

30
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.880
[George] For 112 years, every American president had complied.

31
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:40.640
[George] Sending dry, written messages to be read aloud by monotone clerks, backslash and backslash n on April 8, 1913, Woodrow Wilson stunned Washington by walking directly into the House chamber to address a joint session of Congress in person.

32
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.880
[George] Dressed in a formal cutaway coat, Wilson ascended the dais.

33
00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.320
[George] Looked the astonished senators and congressmen in the eye, and opened with crisp clarity.

34
00:04:50.320 --> 00:05:02.000
[George] I am very glad indeed to have this opportunity to address you in person and verify that the President of the United States is a human being trying to cooperate with other human beings in a common service.

35
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.920
[George] And not a mere department of the government hailing Congress from some isolated island of jealous power.

36
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.800
[George] Wilson had revived a tradition that every modern president follows to this day.

37
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.560
[George] The State of the Union address delivered in person to the nation.

38
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:29.200
[George] Backslash and backslash and by addressing Congress in person, Wilson fundamentally transformed the relationship between the executive and legislative branches, establishing the modern rhetorical presidency.

39
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:37.960
[George] He commanded the national news cycle, framing legislative debates in moral terms that made congressional resistance politically dangerous.

40
00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:42.440
[George] Wilson's opening legislative offensive targeted the protective tariff.

41
00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:49.640
[George] For fifty years, high tariffs had enriched Northern manufacturing monopolies while penalizing consumers and farmers.

42
00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:54.440
[George] Standing before Congress, Wilson demanded sweeping reductions in duties.

43
00:05:54.440 --> 00:06:05.880
[George] When corporate lobbyists descended on Capitol Hill by the hundreds, buying drinks, renting hotel suites, and twisting arms, Wilson went directly over their heads to the American public.

44
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:15.320
[George] In an extraordinary public statement, the president denounced the insidious and numerous lobby that infested Washington, shaming Congress into submission.

45
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:24.920
[George] Backslash N backslash N in October 1913, Congress passed the Landmark Revenue Act of 1913, commonly known as the Underwood Tariff.

46
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:31.000
[George] The law slashed tariffs from 40% to 26% across hundreds of consumer goods.

47
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:41.320
[George] To replace the lost customs revenue, the Act utilized the newly ratified 16th Amendment to institute the first permanent federal income tax in American history.

48
00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:53.480
[George] The tax was progressive, it levied a modest 1% tax on personal incomes above $3,000, rising to a top bracket of 6% on incomes over $500,000.

49
00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:57.400
[George] Less than 1% of the wealthiest Americans paid the tax.

50
00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:14.440
[George] Establishing an enduring foundation for modern federal revenue, backslash and backslash n the Underwood tariff was a landmark victory for working Americans, ending half a century of monopolistic tariff protection and establishing a progressive income tax based on the ability to pay.

51
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:23.680
[George] Wilson's direct public appeal against corporate lobbyists broke the power of special interests and restored democratic accountability to tax policy.

52
00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:31.200
[George] Wilson's greatest and most enduring legislative achievement was the creation of the modern American central banking system.

53
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:42.560
[George] For nearly a century, ever since Andrew Jackson had destroyed the second bank of the United States, the American economy had suffered from an inelastic currency and recurrent banking panics.

54
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:56.560
[George] When crops were harvested in the autumn, cash was sucked out of commercial banks to pay farmers, precipitating severe liquidity shortages, bank runs, and depressions like the Panic of 1893 and 1907.

55
00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.200
[George] Demanded a single privately controlled central bank in New York.

56
00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:16.560
[George] Agrarian populists, led by Secretary of State William Jennings Bryan, demanded a decentralized system controlled entirely by the federal government.

57
00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.080
[George] Wilson forged a brilliant, historic compromise.

58
00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:40.960
[George] Working with Virginia Congressman Carter Glass, Wilson designed the Federal Reserve Act of 1913, a decentralized system of twelve regional Federal Reserve banks scattered across the country, owned by their member private banks, but supervised and governed by a public presidential appointed Federal Reserve Board in Washington.

59
00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:54.320
[George] Backslash and backslash and the Federal Reserve created a flexible national currency, Federal Reserve Notes, that expanded and contracted with commercial demand, ending the seasonal liquidity panics of the 19th century.

60
00:08:54.320 --> 00:09:04.680
[George] When Wilson signed the bill into law two days before Christmas in 1913, he created the foundational financial architecture that governs the modern global economy.

61
00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:17.240
[George] Backslash and backslash and the Federal Reserve Act ended the recurring financial panics of the 19th century by creating a flexible, decentralized national currency under public democratic control.

62
00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:26.200
[George] The twelve regional banks ensured that Western and Southern farmers were no longer held hostage by the liquidity shortages of Wall Street financial houses.

63
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.760
[George] Wilson did not stop with banking.

64
00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:38.040
[George] In 1914, he tackled corporate monopoly with the passage of the Clayton Antitrust Act and the creation of the Federal Trade Commission, FTC.

65
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:54.600
[George] The Clayton Act dramatically strengthened the Sherman Antitrust Act, outlawing predatory price discrimination, interlocking corporate directorates, and tying agreements that forced retailers to boycott competing goods, backslash and backslash and most importantly.

66
00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:03.720
[George] The Clayton Act included a historic provision hailed by American Federation of Labor President Samuel Gompers as the Magna Carta of American labor.

67
00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:16.120
[George] Section 6 of the Act explicitly declared that the labor of a human being is not a commodity or article of commerce, exempting peaceful labor unions and agricultural cooperatives from antitrust prosecution.

68
00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:30.760
[George] Meanwhile, the Federal Trade Commission was established as an independent federal regulatory agency empowered to investigate corporate misconduct, issue cease and desist orders, and halt unfair trade practices before monopolies could form.

69
00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:39.080
[George] In 18 miraculous months, Woodrow Wilson had enacted the most comprehensive progressive reform program in American history.

70
00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:51.040
[George] Backslash and backslash and the Clayton Act's recognition of labor rights and the creation of the federal Trade Commission established essential legal guardrails against corporate monopoly and unfair competition.

71
00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:59.600
[George] Samuel Gompers celebrated the legislation as a historic turning point that recognized the fundamental human dignity of American working people.

72
00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:06.240
[George] Yet Woodrow Wilson's transformative presidency was marred by a profound and tragic moral blind spot.

73
00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.760
[George] A native Southerner of his era, Wilson brought deep racial prejudices into the White House.

74
00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:33.200
[George] Shortly after taking office, Wilson permitted cabinet secretaries, most notably Postmaster General Albert Burleson and Treasury Secretary William McAdoo to systematically resegregate federal offices, installing screens, separate restrooms, and segregated lunchrooms in federal agencies that had been integrated since Reconstruction.

75
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:43.440
[George] Backslash N backslash N when black civil rights leader William Monroe Trotter led a delegation to the White House to protest the indignity, Wilson lost his temper.

76
00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:50.400
[George] Claiming that segregation was intended to prevent friction and dismissing the civil rights leaders from his office.

77
00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.800
[George] In 1915, Wilson hosted a private White House screening of D.

78
00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:55.040
[George] W.

79
00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:58.640
[George] Griffith's racist epic film, The Birth of a Nation.

80
00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.120
[George] Wilson was a tragic paradox.

81
00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:11.200
[George] An intellectual visionary who revolutionized economic freedom and world diplomacy, yet remained blind to the foundational human rights of his black fellow citizens.

82
00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:18.400
[George] As 1914 came to a close, personal tragedy struck the executive mansion.

83
00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:22.880
[George] Wilson's beloved wife Ellen Axson Wilson died of kidney disease.

84
00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:26.400
[George] The heartbroken president wept at her bedside for hours.

85
00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:33.880
[George] Across the Atlantic, the guns of August had opened fire, plunging Europe into the horrific slaughter of World War I.

86
00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:42.600
[George] The peaceful era of domestic reform was ending, and Woodrow Wilson was about to be thrust into the ultimate crucible of global catastrophe.

87
00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:53.240
[George] Backslash and backslash and Wilson's embrace of federal segregation remained a tragic moral failure that contradicted his soaring rhetoric of democratic freedom and human equality.

88
00:12:53.240 --> 00:13:04.120
[George] By separating federal workers by race, Wilson entrenched Jim Crow practices in the nation's capital, leaving a painful and enduring scar on his progressive legacy.

89
00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:15.320
[Rebecca] Woodrow Wilson's new freedom revolutionized the architecture of American capitalism, creating the Federal Reserve, the income tax, and federal trade regulation that endure to this day.

90
00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:21.000
[Rebecca] Yet his legacy remains deeply complex, shadowed by the tragedy of federal segregation.

91
00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.320
[Rebecca] Next week, we examine Wilson's second great historic challenge.

92
00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:34.040
[Rebecca] His struggle for neutrality, the sinking of the Lusitania, America's entry into World War I, and his tragic crusade for the League of Nations.

93
00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:38.200
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

